          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p There are a number of different, inequivalent, definitions of “cosmos” in the literature.
          %h3#bnabou_cosmoi Bénabou cosmoi
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B%C3%A9nabou+cosmos"} Bénabou cosmos
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Street74"} Street 74, p. 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} complete
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
            (hence
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicomplete+category"} bicomplete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
          %p
            This is the ideal situation for
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
          %h3#indexed_bnabou_cosmoi Indexed Bénabou cosmoi
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Shulman13"} Shulman (2013)
            introduced an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+category"} indexed
            generalization of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B%C3%A9nabou+cosmoi"} Bénabou cosmoi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+categories"} indexed categories
            over a base category. Notably, while the definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B%C3%A9nabou+cosmoi"} Bénabou cosmoi
            is not “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"}> elementary
            ” (it involves infinitary (non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limit"}> finite
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B%C3%A9nabou+cosmoi"} Bénabou cosmoi
            is recovered as particular cosmoi indexed over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %h3#streets_fibrational_cosmoi Street’s “fibrational cosmoi”
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
            has taken a different tack, defining a “cosmos” to be the collection of (enriched) categories and relevant structure for doing category theory, rather than the “base” category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            over which the enrichment occurs.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Street74"} Street (1974)
            defined a
            %strong (fibrational) cosmos
            to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            in which internal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibrations"} fibrations
            are well-behaved and representable by a structure of “presheaf objects” (later realized to be a special sort of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+structure"} Yoneda structure
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %em not
            include
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/VCat"} VCat
            for non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+fibrations"} internal fibrations
            are poorly behaved there. The definition is given in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Street80"} Street (1980)
          %p
            A
            %em fibrational cosmos
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            such that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
                exist in
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %li
              %p
                there is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-adjunction"} 2-adjunction
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi P
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi K
                        %mo ⇆
                        %msup
                          %mi K
                          %mtext coop
                        %mo :
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^* \colon K \leftrightarrows K^{\text{coop}} : P
            %li
              %p
                each object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                admits a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibration"} discrete fibration
                from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P A
                satisfying two technical properties.
          %p
            The objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P A
            are the “presheaf objects” that represent fibrations.
          %h3#streets_second_definition Street’s second definition
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Street81"} Street (1981)
            instead defines a cosmos to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            that “behaves like the 2-category
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            and
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
          %p
            A cosmos is a 2-category (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Small (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-limit"}> weak, or bi-
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
                exist.
            %li
              %p
                Each
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
                admits a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+construction"} Kleisli construction
                (analogous to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exactness
                of a topos).
            %li
              %p
                It is locally small-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"}> cocomplete
                , i.e. its hom-categories have small colimits that are preserved by composition on each side.
            %li
              %p There exists a small “Cauchy generator”.
          %p
            These hypotheses imply that it is equivalent to the bicategory of categories and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            over some “base”
            = succeed "." do
              %em bicategory
          %p
            Defined in this way, cosmoi are closed under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} dualization
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
          %h2#related_notions Related notions
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B%C3%A9nabou+cosmos"} Bénabou cosmos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+of+enrichment"} base of enrichment
            %li
              %p
                An
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-cosmos"} infinity-cosmos
                is a “good place in which to do higher category theory” as axiomatized by Riehl and Verity in their work on the foundations of
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Apparently there is no written account by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+B%C3%A9nabou"} Jean Bénabou
            of his definition of cosmos. One finds it recounted in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Street74"} Street 74, p. 1
          %blockquote
            %p
              “to J. Bénabou the word means ”bicomplete symmetric monoidal category“, such categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
              being rich enough so that the theory of categories enriched in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
              develops to a large extent just as the theory of ordinary categories.”
          %ul
            %li#Street74
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
                ,
                %em Elementary cosmoi I.
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Category Seminar
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 420
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0063103"} doi:10.1007/BFb0063103
            %li#Street80
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cosmoi of internal categories
                %strong 258
                2 (1980) 271-318 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1998059"}> doi:10.2307/1998059
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Street81
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cauchy characterization of enriched categories
                %strong 4
                (2004) 1-16 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/4/tr4abs.html"}> tac:tr4
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/4/tr4.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Shulman13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Enriched indexed categories
                %strong 28
                21 (2013) 616-695 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/28/21/28-21abs.html"}> tac:28-21
                )
        .revisedby
